 of new applications to effective Diophantine approximation and beyond.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"bb4972fc-0738-4963-ab1c-2cf2781e82ff":{"speakerId":"bb4972fc-0738-4963-ab1c-2cf2781e82ff","speakerCategoryId":"cee6986f-c8ec-441f-94a0-81cee775f986","sessionId":"8d0e9e39-b74c-4d9d-be96-e99c31413fb0","sessionSpeakerOrder":2},"f6536f71-617f-4c11-bb3b-4e945b204d07":{"speakerId":"f6536f71-617f-4c11-bb3b-4e945b204d07","speakerCategoryId":"cee6986f-c8ec-441f-94a0-81cee775f986","sessionId":"8d0e9e39-b74c-4d9d-be96-e99c31413fb0","sessionSpeakerOrder":3},"e8566521-9bd1-47e9-b99b-1b2a57a6a030":{"speakerId":"e8566521-9bd1-47e9-b99b-1b2a57a6a030","speakerCategoryId":"cee6986f-c8ec-441f-94a0-81cee775f986","sessionId":"8d0e9e39-b74c-4d9d-be96-e99c31413fb0","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524":{"code":"DengYuSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\"><div class=css-vsf5of><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">A major part of Hilbert’s sixth problem asks for the mathematical justification of the passage from atomistic interactions to the laws of motion of continuum. In the classical particle setting, this corresponds to the well known program of going from Newtonian particle dynamics to fluid equations, via Boltzmann’s kinetic theory. In the wave setting, one starts from nonlinear dispersive equations and aims at deriving the wave kinetic equation, i.e. wave analog of Boltzmann’s equation. In this talk I will present recent works, joint with Zaher Hani and Xiao Ma, that provide answer to both problems (in Boltzmann-Grad limit), starting from hard sphere dynamics for particles, and the cubic nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation for waves. The two proofs follow the same framework (with distinctive technical features), namely the propagation of cumulant formulas, in the form of Feynman diagrammatic expansions.</p></div></div>","id":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524","capacityId":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524","name":"Hilbert's Sixth Problem: Particles and Waves","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","waitlistCapacityId":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-29T19:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-29T19:45:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":60,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524","displayValue":"Yu Deng","answers":["Yu Deng"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524","displayValue":"10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"richTextDescription":"{\"format\":\"draftjs-nucleus\",\"version\":1,\"content\":{\"blocks\":[{\"key\":\"1kfpp\",\"text\":\"A major part of Hilbert’s sixth problem asks for the mathematical justification of the passage from atomistic interactions to the laws of motion of continuum. In the classical particle setting, this corresponds to the well known program of going from Newtonian particle dynamics to fluid equations, via Boltzmann’s kinetic theory. In the wave setting, one starts from nonlinear dispersive equations and aims at deriving the wave kinetic equation, i.e. wave analog of Boltzmann’s equation. In this talk I will present recent works, joint with Zaher Hani and Xiao Ma, that provide answer to both problems (in Boltzmann-Grad limit), starting from hard sphere dynamics for particles, and the cubic nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation for waves. The two proofs follow the same framework (with distinctive technical features), namely the propagation of cumulant formulas, in the form of Feynman diagrammatic expansions.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}","plainTextDescription":"A major part of Hilbert’s sixth problem asks for the mathematical justification of the passage from atomistic interactions to the laws of motion of continuum. In the classical particle setting, this corresponds to the well known program of going from Newtonian particle dynamics to fluid equations, via Boltzmann’s kinetic theory. In the wave setting, one starts from nonlinear dispersive equations and aims at deriving the wave kinetic equation, i.e. wave analog of Boltzmann’s equation. In this talk I will present recent works, joint with Zaher Hani and Xiao Ma, that provide answer to both problems (in Boltzmann-Grad limit), starting from hard sphere dynamics for particles, and the cubic nonlinear Schrödinger (NLS) equation for waves. The two proofs follow the same framework (with distinctive technical features), namely the propagation of cumulant formulas, in the form of Feynman diagrammatic expansions.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"1b8abbe3-e938-4d9a-afc7-f586fc9f4d11":{"speakerId":"1b8abbe3-e938-4d9a-afc7-f586fc9f4d11","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"dbb190e3-deb9-494e-9e13-f21a95972524","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040":{"code":"SCRamKaranChoudharySESSION","description":"In this talk, I will present a combinatorial formula for computing the Wedderburn decomposition of the rational group algebra $\\mathbb{Q}G$, where $G$ is a metacyclic $p$-group for any prime $p$. I will also introduce a formula to count the irreducible rational representations of $G$ with distinct degrees. Furthermore, I will outline a method for explicitly constructing all inequivalent irreducible rational matrix representations of $G$.","id":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040","capacityId":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040","name":"A Combinatorial Technique for Rational Representations and Rational Group Algebras of Metacyclic $p$-groups","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":206,"waitlistCapacityId":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-29T16:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-29T16:50:00.000Z","locationId":"002ab162-a334-43ba-82fb-22118ca06cc8","locationName":"115-C","locationCode":"115-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":5,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040","displayValue":"Ram Karan Choudhary","answers":["Ram Karan Choudhary"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040","displayValue":"2 - Algebra","answers":["2 - Algebra"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"223645e6-03de-4a6a-9d38-ec124d30d6fd":{"speakerId":"223645e6-03de-4a6a-9d38-ec124d30d6fd","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"1ce9d6b6-0d1b-4a53-aad4-72c75d5ba040","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:46:06.753Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"ef3cd665-47ab-44b8-aad2-5e6e20f1de18":{"code":"SCBrahimFahidSESSION","description":"Lie $\\sigma$-derivations on path algebras. Motivated by Benkovič’s works on triangular algebras and by Li and Wei’s studies on derivations of path algebras, we extend these results by removing the faithfulness condition. We establish that every Jordan $\\sigma$-derivation on a path algebra associated with a finite acyclic quiver is a $\\sigma$-derivation, and every Lie $\\sigma$-derivation is of standard form. These findings confirm that, under suitable algebraic constraints, Jordan and Lie $\\sigma$-derivations behave analogously to classical $\\sigma$-derivations, thus generalizing existing results in algebraic structures connected to quivers and representation theory.","id":"ef3cd665-47ab-44b8-aad2-5e6e20f1de18","capacityId":"ef3cd665-47ab-44b8-aad2-5e6e20f1de18","name":"Jordan and Lie σ-Derivations on Path Algebras","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"ef3cd665-47ab-44b8-aad2-5e6e20f1de18_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T15:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T15:30:00.000Z","locationId":"c7fdfa90-a4e4-44bd-ad39-4eb44fb769b5","locationName":"115-A","locationCode":"115-A","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":10,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"ef3cd665-47ab-44b8-aad2-5e6e20f1de18","displayValue":"Brahim Fahid","answers":["Brahim Fa