 likelihood (close line).\r\nInteractive Math Festival\r\nLarge-scale hands-on math festival environment featuring multiple table-based activities designed to spark curiosity, collaboration, and joyful problem-solving for K–8 audiences.\r\nAre We There Yet? An Exploration of Distance\r\nImmersive multi-model math exploration combining physical builds, interactive puzzles, and VR to demonstrate different concepts of distance and geometry across mathematical systems.\r\nMath Card Game Exhibit\r\nInteractive math card game experience connecting middle school math concepts to real-world scenarios through collaborative, play-based problem solving.\r\nSierpinski Simplices\r\nCollaborative fractal-building activity where participants construct a large Sierpinski tetrahedron through hands-on assembly, illustrating patterns, scaling, and mathematical structure.","registrantInformation":"All Full Attendees","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-04-12T12:33:36.373Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e":{"code":"JoshuaZahlSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">A Besicovitch set is a compact subset of Rn that contains a unit line segment pointing in every direction. The Kakeya set conjecture asserts that every Besicovitch set in Rn has Minkowski and Hausdorff dimension n. I will discuss progress on this conjecture, leading to the resolution of the Kakeya set conjecture in three dimensions. This is joint work with Hong Wang.</div>","id":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e","capacityId":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e","name":"The Kakeya Set Conjecture in R^3","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"f9d32c0b-6740-41f5-bba5-c2158abd2b5e","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-28T20:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T20:45:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":66,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e","displayValue":"Joshua Zahl","answers":["Joshua Zahl"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e","displayValue":"5 - Geometry,8 - Analysis,10 - Partial Differential Equations,13 - Combinatorics","answers":["8 - Analysis","5 - Geometry","13 - Combinatorics","10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"richTextDescription":"A Besicovitch set is a compact subset of Rn that contains a unit line segment pointing in every direction. The Kakeya set conjecture asserts that every Besicovitch set in Rn has Minkowski and Hausdorff dimension n. I will discuss progress on this conjecture, leading to the resolution of the Kakeya set conjecture in three dimensions. This is joint work with Hong Wang.","plainTextDescription":"A Besicovitch set is a compact subset of Rn that contains a unit line segment pointing in every direction. The Kakeya set conjecture asserts that every Besicovitch set in Rn has Minkowski and Hausdorff dimension n. I will discuss progress on this conjecture, leading to the resolution of the Kakeya set conjecture in three dimensions. This is joint work with Hong Wang.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"a1dfce82-f5d5-4683-b6c5-5f663fc6a71b":{"speakerId":"a1dfce82-f5d5-4683-b6c5-5f663fc6a71b","speakerCategoryId":"f3225eab-7f79-4339-a1f2-c5e465b01f72","sessionId":"72c8cd89-83a0-46c3-803e-dbd9c650476e","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"c0dece90-7404-44da-93e0-2201595ec72f":{"code":"SCKOUAKOUGERMAINBROUSESSION","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Hypergroups generalize locally compact groups. They appear when the Banach space of all bounded Radon measures on a locally compact space carries a convolution having all properties of a group convolution apart from the fact that the convolution of two point measures is a probability measure with compact support and not necessarily a point measure. The intention was to unify harmonic analysis on duals of compact groups, double coset spaces $%G//H $ where $H$ is a compact subgroup of a locally compact group $G$, and commutative convolution algebras associated with product linearization formulas of special functions. Harmonic analysis and probability theory on commutative hypergroups are well developed meanwhile where many results from group theory remain valid. When $G$ is a commutative hypergroup, the convolution algebra <code></code>$M_{c}(G)$ consisting of measures with compact support on $G$ is commutative. If the hypergroup $G$ is not commutative, it is possible to involve a compact subhypergroup $K$ of $G$ leading to a commutative subalgebra of <code></code>$M_{c}(G)$, in this case, the pair $(G,K)$ is said to be a Gelfand pair.In this work, considering a Gelfand pair $(G,K)$, we define the inverse Fourier transform on the algebra $M_{b}(\\widehat{G})$ consisting of bounded measures on the dual space $\\widehat{G}$ of $G$. We then define the positive (resp. strongly positive) definite measure (resp. function) on $\\widehat{G}$ . Finally, we prove that for any strongly positive definite function on $\\widehat{G}$, there exists a bounded positive measure on $G$ whose Fourier transform coincides with this function on the support of the Plancherelmeasure. This result is of Bochner type.</div>","id":"c0dece90-7404-44da-93e0-2201595ec72f","capacityId":"c0dece90-7404-44da-93e0-2201595ec72f","name":"XXX CANCELLED A Bochner Theorem on the Dual Space of a Hypergroup.","status":7,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"c0dece90-7404-44da-93e0-2201595ec72f_waitlist","dataTagCode":"XXX CANCELLED A Bochner T","startTime":"2026-07-28T14:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T15:10:00.000Z","locationId":"18f6e81f-a563-4e79-a907-872c0f1b59a7","locationName":"119-AB","locationCode":"119-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":0,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"c0dece90-7404-44da-93e0-2201595ec72f","displayValue":"8 - Analysis","answers":["8 - Analysis"]}},"richTextDescription":"Hypergroups generalize locally compact groups. They appear when the Banach space of all bounded Radon measures on a locally compact space carries a convolution having all properties of a group convolution apart from the fact that the convolution of two point measures is a probability measure with compact support and not necessarily a point measure. The intention was to unify harmonic analysis on duals of compact groups, double coset spaces $%G//H $ where $H$ is a compact subgroup of a locally compact group $G$, and commutative convolution algebras associated with product linearization formulas of special functions. Harmonic analysis and probability theory on commutative hypergroups are well developed meanwhile where many results from group theory remain valid. When $G$ is a commutative hypergroup, the convolution algebra <code></cod