sciplinary nature.</p></div></div>","id":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916","capacityId":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916","name":"Bridges to Translational Tilings: From Domino to p-adic Sudoku","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"f9d32c0b-6740-41f5-bba5-c2158abd2b5e","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-29T20:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-29T20:45:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":44,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916","displayValue":"Rachel Greenfeld","answers":["Rachel Greenfeld"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916","displayValue":"1 - Logic,2 - Algebra,3 - Number Theory,5 - Geometry,8 - Analysis,9 - Dynamics,13 - Combinatorics","answers":["8 - Analysis","9 - Dynamics","2 - Algebra","5 - Geometry","13 - Combinatorics","1 - Logic","3 - Number Theory"]}},"richTextDescription":"{\"format\":\"draftjs-nucleus\",\"version\":1,\"content\":{\"blocks\":[{\"key\":\"evr80\",\"text\":\"The study of the structure of translational tilings has captivated mathematicians, scientists, and the general public for centuries and continues to thrive at the crossroads of analysis, combinatorics, dynamics, logic, number theory, and geometry. This vibrant field seeks to uncover the delicate divide between rigid structures and unpredictable, \\\"wild\\\" behaviors that arise when sets fill space by translations without gaps or overlaps. I will provide an overview of this study and discuss recent developments, highlighting its interdisciplinary nature.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}","plainTextDescription":"The study of the structure of translational tilings has captivated mathematicians, scientists, and the general public for centuries and continues to thrive at the crossroads of analysis, combinatorics, dynamics, logic, number theory, and geometry. This vibrant field seeks to uncover the delicate divide between rigid structures and unpredictable, \"wild\" behaviors that arise when sets fill space by translations without gaps or overlaps. I will provide an overview of this study and discuss recent developments, highlighting its interdisciplinary nature.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"57ebcd5c-42f9-4f41-a667-b1fcaaae417f":{"speakerId":"57ebcd5c-42f9-4f41-a667-b1fcaaae417f","speakerCategoryId":"f3225eab-7f79-4339-a1f2-c5e465b01f72","sessionId":"61ff0fba-ae97-45c1-8aca-eb1f27d7e916","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"3159e542-85e4-4b42-bcb5-03a001af3e0a":{"code":"PLLaudatio7","description":"","id":"3159e542-85e4-4b42-bcb5-03a001af3e0a","capacityId":"3159e542-85e4-4b42-bcb5-03a001af3e0a","name":"HIDDEN Leelavati Prize Laudatio: Carola-Bibiane Schönlieb","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"12c75c27-5978-4dc7-a8f8-d333f59ab257","waitlistCapacityId":"3159e542-85e4-4b42-bcb5-03a001af3e0a_waitlist","dataTagCode":"Leelavati Prize Laudatio:","startTime":"2026-07-23T20:55:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-23T21:15:00.000Z","locationId":"44c8f801-3f99-48fd-b56a-4e40543f2a0f","locationName":"Terrace Ballroom","locationCode":"Terrace Ballroom","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":0,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":false,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"472df1ef-81e7-472e-841e-7307e9678a23":{"speakerId":"472df1ef-81e7-472e-841e-7307e9678a23","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"3159e542-85e4-4b42-bcb5-03a001af3e0a","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-06-29T18:28:26.417Z","restrictSessionVisibility":false},"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff":{"code":"SCCGopalakrishnaSESSION","description":"An iterative root of order $n\\ge 2$ of a self-map $f$ on a nonempty set is a self-map $g$ on the set such that $g^n=f$. Most existing results on the iterative roots of continuous interval maps rely on tools such as the Intermediate Value Theorem and the monotonicity of bijections. Extending these results to higher dimensions or more general topological spaces is generally complex.  In this talk we present some new combinatorial ideas that lead to new results on iterative roots of maps on arbitrary sets and continuous maps on topological spaces. These results, in particular, allow us to generalize some notable findings on iterative roots for continuous interval maps to the broader context of continuous multidimensional maps. The talk is based on two recent joint works with B. V. Rajarama Bhat.","id":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff","capacityId":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff","name":"Some Combinatorial Approaches to the Nonexistence of Iterative Roots","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":706,"waitlistCapacityId":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T17:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T17:50:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":10,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff","displayValue":"Chaitanya Gopalakrishna","answers":["Chaitanya Gopalakrishna"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff","displayValue":"9 - Dynamics","answers":["9 - Dynamics"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"e7591486-a5da-4668-a59c-cfdcbb3c6a55":{"speakerId":"e7591486-a5da-4668-a59c-cfdcbb3c6a55","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"bf99090c-da54-423b-a754-eeb537eb92ff","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19":{"code":"SCZoranMisajleskiSESSION","description":"Let $\\mathcal{U}$ be an open covering of the topological space $X$ in $X$, $C\\subseteq X$ and $x,y\\in X$. A chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$ is a finite sequence of elements of $\\mathcal{U}$ such that $x$ belongs to the first element of the sequence, $y$ to the last, and intersection of any two consecutive elements of the chain is nonempty. The set $C$ is totally weakly chain separated in $X$ if for every two distinct points $x,y\\in C$ there exists an open covering $\\mathcal{U}=\\mathcal{U}_{xy}$ of $X$ such that there is no chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$. The set $C$ is totally chain separated in $X$ if there exists an open covering $\\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ such that for every two distinct points $x,y\\in C$ there is no chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$. The set $C$ is C-chain connected in $X$, if for every clopen (closed and open) covering $\\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ and every $x,y\\in C$, there exists a chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$. Let $A$ and $B$ be nonempty subsets of $X$. The sets $A$ and $B$ are C-weakly chain separated in $X$, if for every point $x\\in A$ and every $y\\in B$, there exists a clopen covering $\\mathcal{U}=\\mathcal{U}_{xy}$ of $X$ such that there is no chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$. The sets $A$ and $B$ are C-chain separated in $X$, if there exists a clopen covering $\\mathcal{U}$ of $X$ such that for every point $x\\in X$ and every $y\\in Y$, there is no chain in $\\mathcal{U}$ that connects $x$ and $y$. In this presentation we will study the properties of those sets. We will formulate statements in which the notions of C-chain connectedness, homotopy, and shape appear. Furthermore we will define analog notions for one previously given open or clopen covering. At the end we will define analog notions in a more general space then a topological.","id":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19","capacityId":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19","name":"C-Chain Connectedness","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-24T16:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-24T16:50:00.000Z","locationId":"53df18b7-15a9-4d2e-8f2c-9445f7309783","locationName":"115-B","locationCode":"115-B","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":6,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19","displayValue":"Zoran Misajleski","answers":["Zoran Misajleski"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19","displayValue":"6 - Topology","answers":["6 - Topology"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"66d3333f-80db-47fb-bf5b-1cc7188b41eb":{"speakerId":"66d3333f-80db-47fb-bf5b-1cc7188b41eb","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"814d6294-37ba-4051-9002-6b9c68a7ed19","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"1f839290-c31c-4299-9965-208ce13f7653":{"code":"SCAshishBawaliaSESSION","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">We establish the global well-posedness of the $D(A)-$valued strong solution to a nonlinear heat equation with constraints on a Poincaré domain $\\mathcal{O}\\subset \\mathbb{R}^d$ whose boundary is of class $C^2$. Consider the following nonlinear heat equation\\begin{align*}   \\frac{\\partial u}{\\partial t} - \\Delta u + |u|^{p-2}u = 0,\\end{align*}projected onto the tangent space $T_u\\mathcal{M}$, where $\\mathcal{M}:=\\left\\{u\\in  L^2(\\mathcal{O}):\\|u\\|_{L^2(\\mathcal{O})}=1\\right\\}$ is a submanifold of $L^2(\\mathcal{O})$. The  nonlinearity exponent satisfies $2\\le p < \\infty$ for $1\\leq d\\leq 4$ and  $2 \\le p \\le \\frac{2d-4}{d-4}$ for $d \\ge 5$. The solution is constrained to lie within $\\mathcal{M}$, which encodes the norm-preserving constraint. By modifying the nonlinearity and exploiting the abstract theory for $m-$accretive evolution equations, we prove the existence of a global strong solution. Using the resolvent-idea and the Yosida approximation method, we derive regularity results. In the asymptotic analysis, $\\mathcal{O}$ is restricted to bounded domains with even $p$ and $1\\le d \\le 3$. For any initial data in $D(A) \\cap \\mathcal{M}$, we apply the Łojasiewicz-Simon gradient inequality on a Hilbert submanifold, to demonstrate that the unique global strong solution converges in $W^{2,q}(\\mathcal{O}) \\cap W^{1,q}_0(\\mathcal{O})$ to a stationary state, where $2 \\le q < \\frac{2d}{d + 4 - 4\\beta}$ and $1 < \\beta < \\frac{3}{2}$. This work proposes an alternative method for establishing the global existence and analyzing long-term behavior of the unique strong solution to an $L^2-$norm preserving nonlinear heat equation.It is a joint work with Zdzisław Brzeźniak, Manil T. Mohan and Piotr Rybka.</div>","id":"1f839290-c31c-4299-9965-208ce13f7653","capacityId":"1f839290-c31c-4299-9965-208ce13f7653","name":"Well-Posedness and the ŁOjasiewicz-Simon Inequality in the Asymptotic Analysis of a Nonlinear Heat Equation with Constraints of Finite Codimension","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"1f839290-c31c-4299-9965-208ce13f7653_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-27T16:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-27T16:50:00.000Z","locationId":"18f6e81f-a563-4e79-a907-872c0f1b59a7","locationName":"119-AB","locationCode":"119-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":6,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"1f839290-c31c-4299-9965-208ce13f7653","displayValue"