cations of area-minimization to the study of curvature in Riemannian geometry.</div>","id":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787","capacityId":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787","name":"Minimal Surfaces and Comparison Geometry","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","waitlistCapacityId":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787_waitlist","dataTagCode":"Minimal Surfaces and Comp","startTime":"2026-07-28T20:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T20:45:00.000Z","locationId":"ea2605a7-c03f-482d-8e86-0735611ed089","locationName":"121-AB","locationCode":"121-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":38,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787","displayValue":"Otis Chodosh","answers":["Otis Chodosh"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787","displayValue":"5 - Geometry,10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["5 - Geometry","10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"richTextDescription":"We discuss applications of area-minimization to the study of curvature in Riemannian geometry.","plainTextDescription":"We discuss applications of area-minimization to the study of curvature in Riemannian geometry.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"b1229eef-4e64-4d45-84e4-9bdf78ff1f23":{"speakerId":"b1229eef-4e64-4d45-84e4-9bdf78ff1f23","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"22ae10b1-c56a-41e9-94eb-b8a69779c787","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932":{"code":"SCKathrynHaymakerSESSION","description":"In this communication, we introduce a family of algebraically constructed hierarchical quasi-cyclic codes. These codes are built from Reed-Solomon and polynomial evaluation codes using a 1964 construction of superimposed codes by Kautz and Singleton. Using a novel ordering of the codewords and evaluation points, we show both the number of levels in the hierarchy and the index of these q-ary-derived codes are determined by the field size. We compute explicit code parameters and properties as well as some additional bounds on parameters such as rank and distance. In particular, starting with Reed-Solomon codes of dimension two yields hierarchical quasi-cyclic codes with Tanner graphs of girth 6. Finally, we present a table of small code parameters and note that some of these codes meet the best known minimum distance for binary codes, with the additional hierarchical quasi-cyclic structure.","id":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932","capacityId":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932","name":"Hierarchical Quasi-Cyclic Codes from Reed-Solomon and Polynomial Evaluation Codes","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":269,"waitlistCapacityId":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T14:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T15:10:00.000Z","locationId":"832094e7-0e57-4e55-8e2e-d69b459e2551","locationName":"116-A","locationCode":"116-A","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":8,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932","displayValue":"Kathryn Haymaker","answers":["Kathryn Haymaker"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932","displayValue":"14 - Mathematics of Computer Science","answers":["14 - Mathematics of Computer Science"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"d318499b-4e67-46e8-aa29-869d72c6e844":{"speakerId":"d318499b-4e67-46e8-aa29-869d72c6e844","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"a4bfbd23-59b8-4c15-8e83-d9a89019a932","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00":{"code":"PatrickFarrellSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Many systems of nonlinear equations admit multiple solutions, which are often crucial for understanding the problem at hand.  Algorithms for computing multiple solutions have been developed over several decades in the context of numerical bifurcation analysis, with great success. However, the core algorithms of continuation, bifurcation detection, and branch switching can miss branches of solutions not connected to the given initial solution. They can also be complex to apply to large-scale discretisations of the nonlinear partial differential equations that arise in many real-world problems. A complementary alternative is to employ deflation, which modifies the nonlinear problem so that the Newton--Kantorovich iteration cannot rediscover known solutions, and thus if the iteration converges it finds a new solution. Deflation enables the discovery of disconnected branches. Moreover, when combined with natural or tangent continuation, the resulting subproblems can be efficiently solved if a good preconditioner is available for the original Newton step, making the method convenient and practical to apply at large scale. In this talk we introduce deflation, discuss how it may be combined with classical approaches to yield effective algorithms for discovering multiple solutions of partial differential equations and variational inequalities, and outline some open problems.</div>","id":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00","capacityId":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00","name":"Computing Multiple Solutions of Systems of Nonlinear Equations with Deflation","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","capacity":629,"waitlistCapacityId":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-27T20:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-27T20:45:00.000Z","locationId":"01c33352-9c21-48c8-9c87-4a12ea950167","locationName":"118-AB","locationCode":"118-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":18,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00","displayValue":"Patrick Farrell","answers":["Patrick Farrell"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00","displayValue":"15 - Numerical Analysis and Scientific Computing","answers":["15 - Numerical Analysis and Scientific Computing"]}},"richTextDescription":"Many systems of nonlinear equations admit multiple solutions, which are often crucial for understanding the problem at hand.  Algorithms for computing multiple solutions have been developed over several decades in the context of numerical bifurcation analysis, with great success. However, the core algorithms of continuation, bifurcation detection, and branch switching can miss branches of solutions not connected to the given initial solution. They can also be complex to apply to large-scale discretisations of the nonlinear partial differential equations that arise in many real-world problems. A complementary alternative is to employ deflation, which modifies the nonlinear problem so that the Newton--Kantorovich iteration cannot rediscover known solutions, and thus if the iteration converges it finds a new solution. Deflation enables the discovery of disconnected branches. Moreover, when combined with natural or tangent continuation, the resulting subproblems can be efficiently solved if a good preconditioner is available for the original Newton step, making the method convenient and practical to apply at large scale. In this talk we introduce deflation, discuss how it may be combined with classical approaches to yield effective algorithms for discovering multiple solutions of partial differential equations and variational inequalities, and outline some open problems.","plainTextDescription":"Many systems of nonlinear equations admit multiple solutions, which are often crucial for understanding the problem at hand.  Algorithms for computing multiple solutions have been developed over several decades in the context of numerical bifurcation analysis, with great success. However, the core algorithms of continuation, bifurcation detection, and branch switching can miss branches of solutions not connected to the given initial solution. They can also be complex to apply to large-scale discretisations of the nonlinear partial differential equations that arise in many real-world problems. A complementary alternative is to employ deflation, which modifies the nonlinear problem so that the Newton--Kantorovich iteration cannot rediscover known solutions, and thus if the iteration converges it finds a new solution. Deflation enables the discovery of disconnected branches. Moreover, when combined with natural or tangent continuation, the resulting subproblems can be efficiently solved if a good preconditioner is available for the original Newton step, making the method convenient and practical to apply at large scale. In this talk we introduce deflation, discuss how it may be combined with classical approaches to yield effective algorithms for discovering multiple solutions of partial differential equations and variational inequalities, and outline some open problems.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"4b28efdc-5063-41ef-a894-e61f55361cae":{"speakerId":"4b28efdc-5063-41ef-a894-e61f55361cae","speakerCategoryId":"9885dda8-db49-49b2-ba26-ef4b3da8f7cf","sessionId":"ee99b5b4-5000-441b-bb10-d1a5eab64d00","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"0351a929-236a-4a81-97a7-c7cf6a1f5bd3":{"code":"DzmitryDudkoSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\"><div class=css-vsf5of><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">The MLC Conjecture (the Mandelbrot set is Locally Connected) describes how quadratic polynomials depend topologically on a parameter. The Conjecture has many geometric and probabilistic counterparts; all of them are the subject of the Renormalization Theory, which analyzes first-return maps to small neighborhoods of special points. In this talk, we outline the current state of this theory, with particular emphasis on the (near-) zero-entropy or Molecule (satellite and neutral) regimes.</p></div></div>","id":"0351a929-236a-4a81-97a7-c7cf6a1f5bd3","capacityId":"0351a929-236a-4a81-97a7-c7cf6a1f5bd3","name":"On the MLC Conjecture and the Renormalization Theory in Complex Dynamics","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonTy