ds: that is, approximations of K-theoryby more computable invariants (such as Hochschild homology). In this talk, I'll describe a (conjectural) extension of this methodology to other cohomological invariants in algebraic geometry.</div>","id":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86","capacityId":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86","name":"Prismatic Stable Homotopy Theory","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd1d2e5f-7591-4908-baa7-572aad4aabe4","waitlistCapacityId":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-27T13:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-27T14:00:00.000Z","locationId":"44c8f801-3f99-48fd-b56a-4e40543f2a0f","locationName":"Terrace Ballroom","locationCode":"Terrace Ballroom","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":68,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","b138550a-fdad-4b93-b3c1-08eca41a841f","5b4c0831-8b9d-484d-bd6c-214c48d235d1","460d73a8-a9a0-4af3-a523-6f426e8da92b","bff6831d-6419-4dbc-a0ed-839f5da7edfe","9e68476d-7aaa-434f-aa89-ad258c9770d3","c3465557-42c5-4bd0-8672-db0f026639c8","5fc38934-8fb8-4dd0-9691-f0da68e36cd9"],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86","displayValue":"Jacob Lurie","answers":["Jacob Lurie"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86","displayValue":"","answers":[]}},"richTextDescription":"One of the most powerful approaches to the study of algebraic K-theoryis the use of trace methods: that is, approximations of K-theoryby more computable invariants (such as Hochschild homology). In this talk, I'll describe a (conjectural) extension of this methodology to other cohomological invariants in algebraic geometry.","plainTextDescription":"One of the most powerful approaches to the study of algebraic K-theoryis the use of trace methods: that is, approximations of K-theoryby more computable invariants (such as Hochschild homology). In this talk, I'll describe a (conjectural) extension of this methodology to other cohomological invariants in algebraic geometry.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"c619a290-7611-4053-9d39-96554ca4b9ee":{"speakerId":"c619a290-7611-4053-9d39-96554ca4b9ee","speakerCategoryId":"9885dda8-db49-49b2-ba26-ef4b3da8f7cf","sessionId":"45484157-b5d2-41d9-9351-1161f7329f86","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d":{"code":"SCChristianBuddeSESSION","description":"In this talk we present time-dependent perturbations of second-order non-autonomous abstract Cauchy problems associated to a family of operators with constant domain. We make use of the equivalence to a first-order non-autonomous abstract Cauchy problem in a product space, which we elaborate in full detail. As an application, we provide a perturbed non-autonomous wave equation. This talk is based on joint work with Christian Seifert (TU Hamburg, Germany).","id":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d","capacityId":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d","name":"Perturbations of Non-Autonomous Second-Order Abstract Cauchy Problems","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T14:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T15:10:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":13,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d","displayValue":"Christian Budde","answers":["Christian Budde"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d","displayValue":"10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"f5ee73a4-e06f-4302-9313-e264061f5b14":{"speakerId":"f5ee73a4-e06f-4302-9313-e264061f5b14","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"65e0f3df-fc9d-4b65-b7bb-2ada23abd59d","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9":{"code":"SCPedroEspinosaSESSION","description":"The notion of homological ideals was introduced by M. Auslander et al. in 1992; they referred to them as strong idempotent ideals. These ideals exhibit several interesting properties and have been further studied by Gatica, Lanzilotta, and Platzeck to explore their connections with the Finitistic Dimension Conjecture. In this talk, we will describe some of the main properties of these ideals, and we will generalize ideas given in 2023 by A. Moreno Cañadas and P. F. Fernández Espinosa to provide a combinatorial description of the number of homological ideals in Auslander algebras of radical-square-zero Dynkin algebras.","id":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9","capacityId":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9","name":"Homological Ideals Over Auslander Algebras of Radical Square Zero Algebras of Dynkin Quiver","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T16:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T16:30:00.000Z","locationId":"bc81cad2-8b75-45cb-9355-a764c88805b2","locationName":"120-AB","locationCode":"120-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":12,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9","displayValue":"Pedro Fernando Fernández Espinosa","answers":["Pedro Fernando Fernández Espinosa"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9","displayValue":"2 - Algebra","answers":["2 - Algebra"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"39b5d92b-a428-4d48-b5f0-56911c02cc1a":{"speakerId":"39b5d92b-a428-4d48-b5f0-56911c02cc1a","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"a006421d-b57d-4830-9066-604a0e4783a9","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc":{"code":"GeordieWilliamsonSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\"><div class=css-vsf5of><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">Mathematics has been called humanity's long conversation. Observations of Euclid, Pythagoras, Euler and Poincaré still occupy the minds of mathematicians today. This conversation has experienced shocks and challenges, including the crisis of foundations in the early 20th century, and the first computer assisted proofs in the second half of the 20th century. We are currently in the midst of another shock, with the rise of formal proof and the first signs of AI systems helping to produce research-level mathematics. This raises questions of fundamental importance: How should mathematicians respond to AI? Will AI systems help (or hinder) our understanding of the mathematical world? Williamson will discuss some recent developments at the interface of mathematics and AI, with the aim of having a clearer picture of this unique point in the history of mathematics.</p></div></div>","id":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc","capacityId":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc","name":"AI and Humanity's Long Conversation","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":true,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"50b494df-3069-4f65-bbb9-55e7b3fdef18","capacity":6048,"waitlistCapacityId":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc_waitlist","dataTagCode":"LectureGeordieW","startTime":"2026-07-26T19:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T20:30:00.000Z","locationId":"44c8f801-3f99-48fd-b56a-4e40543f2a0f","locationName":"Terrace Ballroom","locationCode":"Terrace Ballroom","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":1060,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","b138550a-fdad-4b93-b3c1-08eca41a841f","5b4c0831-8b9d-484d-bd6c-214c48d235d1","23f1fb67-bf12-4e91-b1e4-53018783c1cf","eed7f846-6665-4f33-973a-69077e324c7c","460d73a8-a9a0-4af3-a523-6f426e8da92b","bff6831d-6419-4dbc-a0ed-839f5da7edfe","5cb4f53d-aabf-4bc3-b876-8db33cba9ba5","9e68476d-7aaa-434f-aa89-ad258c9770d3","75c39658-1230-4293-815d-ba4009d4c5fc","c3465557-42c5-4bd0-8672-db0f026639c8","5fc38934-8fb8-4dd0-9691-f0da68e36cd9"],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc","displayValue":"Geordie Williamson","answers":["Geordie Williamson"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc","displayValue":"19 - Mathematical Education and Popularization of Mathematics","answers":["19 - Mathematical Education and Popularization of Mathematics"]}},"richTextDescription":"{\"format\":\"draftjs-nucleus\",\"version\":1,\"content\":{\"blocks\":[{\"key\":\"65isd\",\"text\":\"Mathematics has been called humanity's long conversation. Observations of Euclid, Pythagoras, Euler and Poincaré still occupy the minds of mathematicians today. This conversation has experienced shocks and challenges, including the crisis of foundations in the early 20th century, and the first computer assisted proofs in the second half of the 20th century. We are currently in the midst of another shock, with the rise of formal proof and the first signs of AI systems helping to produce research-level mathematics. This raises questions of fundamental importance: How should mathematicians respond to AI? Will AI systems help (or hinder) our understanding of the mathematical world? Williamson will discuss some recent developments at the interface of mathematics and AI, with the aim of having a clearer picture of this unique point in the history of mathematics.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}","plainTextDescription":"Mathematics has been called humanity's long conversation. Observations of Euclid, Pythagoras, Euler and Poincaré still occupy the minds of mathematicians today. This conversation has experienced shocks and challenges, including the crisis of foundations in the early 20th century, and the first computer assisted proofs in the second half of the 20th century. We are currently in the midst of another shock, with the rise of formal proof and the first signs of AI systems helping to produce research-level mathematics. This raises questions of fundamental importance: How should mathematicians respond to AI? Will AI systems help (or hinder) our understanding of the mathematical world? Williamson will discuss some recent developments at the interface of mathematics and AI, with the aim of having a clearer picture of this unique point in the history of mathematics.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"33dbeceb-4393-4d95-a325-541bf0618db3":{"speakerId":"33dbeceb-4393-4d95-a325-541bf0618db3","speakerCategoryId":"2f7495be-22b8-45e6-bf74-aaec3887a0da","sessionId":"ac53d9d2-e30f-4536-8fa9-7713704701dc","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-10-04T09:07:49.363Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8":{"code":"SCClaudiaLedermanSESSION","description":"Non-standard growth PDEs constitute a broad class of singular and degenerate nonlinear equations used in the modeling of inhomogeneous and anisotropic media. Their applications range from modern material science to image restoration and temperature sensor design.We will present recent results on two-phase free boundary problems governed by  operators with non-standard growth. We focus on viscosity solutions to  free boundary problems for  nonlinear elliptic PDEs with  non-zero right hand side and obtain regularity results for solutions and their free boundaries.More precisely, under suitable flatness assumptions we prove that free boundaries are $C^{1,\\gamma}$.In addition, we obtain the Lipschitz continuity of viscosity solutions without  relying on a monotonicity formula.The interest in these problems stems from the diverse applications that motivate their study and from the challenging difficulties they pose due to their singular / degenerate nature.This is joint work with Fausto Ferrari (University of Bologna, Italy).","id":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8","capacityId":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8","name":"Regularity Results for Two-Phase Free Boundary Problems for Non-Standard Growth Equations with Right Hand Side","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T17:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T17:30:00.000Z","locationId":"01c33352-9c21-48c8-9c87-4a12ea950167","locationName":"118-AB","locationCode":"118-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":9,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8","displayValue":"Claudia Lederman","answers":["Claudia Lederman"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8","displayValue":"10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"90dc7e70-cfbf-4009-9615-8683deaeed52":{"speakerId":"90dc7e70-cfbf-4009-9615-8683deaeed52","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"80d0df71-32c6-44e4-8bb4-eb9be2cef9f8","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"fdc96ed0-5395-4d96-942d-beb839de2ccf":{"code":"ICMFILM2","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\"><div class=\"css-vsf5of\"><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">Film Directed by Josef Gatti</p><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">PHENOMENA is a psychedelic odyssey into the fabric of the universe, guided by a filmmaker and his immersive practical experiments that blur the lines between art and science to reveal nature’s inner workings. Captured entirely by camera with no visual effects or artifice, the unfolding awe-inspiring hyper-real imagery is elevated by music from legendary Nils Frahm and an ethereal electronic original score by Rival Consoles, transforming curious observation into a hypnotic audio-visual adventure spanning from the subatomic to cosmological scales. Tracing the forces and elements shaping the natural world, PHENOMENA is a kinetic sensory experience, exploring the wonders of the universe and our connection to it.</p></div></div>","id":"fdc96ed0-5395-4d96-942d-beb839de2ccf","capacityId":"fdc96ed0-5395-4d96-942d-beb839de2ccf","name":"PHENOMENA","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"749da546-7c7d-4d1d-9f6b-e44e7cf5dd74","capacity":150,"waitlistCapacityId":"fdc96ed0-5395-4d96-942d-beb839de2ccf_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T23:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-27T02:00:00.000Z","locationId":"6c27d215-6704-46c4-a72f-5e0f49f354d5","locationName":"Michael A. Nutter Theater","locationCode":"Michael A. Nutter Theater","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":55,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":["5cb4f53d-aabf-4bc3-b876-8db33cba9ba5"],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"c82e91fc-da7a-4a71-a7d5-32011a7a9b41":{"id":"c82e91fc-da7a-4a71-a7d5-32011a7a9b41"