 is bounded with a bound that is independent of the contrast, and include selected numerical experiments that confirm the robustness of our preconditioner.","id":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084","capacityId":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084","name":"Adaptive Coarse Spaces for High-Contrast and Multiscale Coefficients for Problems in H(grad)","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":269,"waitlistCapacityId":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-28T15:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T15:50:00.000Z","locationId":"832094e7-0e57-4e55-8e2e-d69b459e2551","locationName":"116-A","locationCode":"116-A","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":6,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084","displayValue":"Juan Gabriel Calvo Alpizar","answers":["Juan Gabriel Calvo Alpizar"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084","displayValue":"15 - Numerical Analysis and Scientific Computing","answers":["15 - Numerical Analysis and Scientific Computing"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"ae1eedf0-3cc8-46d8-aa8e-24f3ae939d38":{"speakerId":"ae1eedf0-3cc8-46d8-aa8e-24f3ae939d38","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"cf3dbfae-16b3-4f0c-afb9-0d1410426084","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:46:06.753Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67":{"code":"GabrielNavarroSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Two of the most important conjectures in the representation theory of finite groups have been recently proven: Richard Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture (1955) and John McKay’s Degree Conjecture (1971). The proofs of both rely on the Classification of Finite Simple Groups. A key idea in their resolution has been to associate, to each irreducible character of a simple group, another character of a local subgroup that, remarkably, behaves in a corresponding way. This was proposed by M. Isaacs, G. Malle and this author in 2007.In the case of the McKay Conjecture, it took nearly 20 years to fully verify this correspondence, a milestone finally achieved by M. Cabanes and B. Späth. A similar strategy was applied to Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture for the prime 2 by L. Ruhstorfer,  after the so called Gluck--Wolf--Navarro--Tiep theorem was proved, and a reduction of B. Späth and this author.   For odd primes, however, G. Malle, A. Schaeffer-Fry, P. H. Tiep, and the present author had to develop an entirely different approach to finally establish the result.This idea of character correspondents is now being extended to tackle other global/local conjectures, including Alperin’s Weight Conjecture, several refinements of the McKay Conjecture, and even Feit’s Conjecture on fields of values. The purpose of this talk is to explain these developments in greater detail.</div>","id":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67","capacityId":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67","name":"Character Correspondents in Finite Groups","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-24T20:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-24T20:45:00.000Z","locationId":"c7fdfa90-a4e4-44bd-ad39-4eb44fb769b5","locationName":"115-A","locationCode":"115-A","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":27,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67","displayValue":"Gabriel Navarro","answers":["Gabriel Navarro"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67","displayValue":"2 - Algebra","answers":["2 - Algebra"]}},"richTextDescription":"Two of the most important conjectures in the representation theory of finite groups have been recently proven: Richard Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture (1955) and John McKay’s Degree Conjecture (1971). The proofs of both rely on the Classification of Finite Simple Groups. A key idea in their resolution has been to associate, to each irreducible character of a simple group, another character of a local subgroup that, remarkably, behaves in a corresponding way. This was proposed by M. Isaacs, G. Malle and this author in 2007.In the case of the McKay Conjecture, it took nearly 20 years to fully verify this correspondence, a milestone finally achieved by M. Cabanes and B. Späth. A similar strategy was applied to Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture for the prime 2 by L. Ruhstorfer,  after the so called Gluck--Wolf--Navarro--Tiep theorem was proved, and a reduction of B. Späth and this author.   For odd primes, however, G. Malle, A. Schaeffer-Fry, P. H. Tiep, and the present author had to develop an entirely different approach to finally establish the result.This idea of character correspondents is now being extended to tackle other global/local conjectures, including Alperin’s Weight Conjecture, several refinements of the McKay Conjecture, and even Feit’s Conjecture on fields of values. The purpose of this talk is to explain these developments in greater detail.","plainTextDescription":"Two of the most important conjectures in the representation theory of finite groups have been recently proven: Richard Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture (1955) and John McKay’s Degree Conjecture (1971). The proofs of both rely on the Classification of Finite Simple Groups. A key idea in their resolution has been to associate, to each irreducible character of a simple group, another character of a local subgroup that, remarkably, behaves in a corresponding way. This was proposed by M. Isaacs, G. Malle and this author in 2007.In the case of the McKay Conjecture, it took nearly 20 years to fully verify this correspondence, a milestone finally achieved by M. Cabanes and B. Späth. A similar strategy was applied to Brauer’s Height Zero Conjecture for the prime 2 by L. Ruhstorfer,  after the so called Gluck--Wolf--Navarro--Tiep theorem was proved, and a reduction of B. Späth and this author.   For odd primes, however, G. Malle, A. Schaeffer-Fry, P. H. Tiep, and the present author had to develop an entirely different approach to finally establish the result.This idea of character correspondents is now being extended to tackle other global/local conjectures, including Alperin’s Weight Conjecture, several refinements of the McKay Conjecture, and even Feit’s Conjecture on fields of values. The purpose of this talk is to explain these developments in greater detail.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"5a3f87e4-4797-4c21-9a4f-70c6b132ae34":{"speakerId":"5a3f87e4-4797-4c21-9a4f-70c6b132ae34","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"9d494cd4-4493-4e8e-b33a-172c6ab6fe67","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4":{"code":"SCAnhDoSESSION","description":"Quantitative stability for functional and geometric inequalities plays a significant role in the calculus of variations and evolution partial differential equations. Once the set of all optimizers is fully characterized, natural questions on stability results arise about estimating the distance to the manifold of optimizers via the deficit, which is the difference between the two sides of the inequality. Such stability results have wide-ranging applications, including the justification of Taylor expansions and spectral estimates in mathematical physics, as well as a posterior error analysis in numerical methods.Since the foundational work of Bianchi and Egnell, there has been growing interest in developing quantitative stability results. In this talk, we present recent advances in the study of the stability for Caffarelli–Kohn–Nirenberg (CKN) inequalities, which encompass and generalize several classical inequalities, including Sobolev and Gagliardo–Nirenberg inequalities. We first provide a complete classification of $L^2$-stability results for all parameter regimes, extending and completing earlier work by Cazacu, Flynn, Lam, and Lu. In addition, using an alternative method that avoids spherical harmonics decomposition, we establish new $L^p$-stability results. Motivated by recent work of Dolbeault, Esteban, Figalli, Frank, and Loss, the sharpness of stability constants has emerged as a central topic. Addressing an open question posed by Maz'ya, we obtain sharp stability results for the Heisenberg Uncertainty Principle (HUP) in the setting of second-order and curl-free vector fields. To further generalize these findings, we derive new $L^2$-stability results for HUP by introducing several weighted Poincaré inequalities with Gaussian measures on hyperbolic spaces, where classical scaling arguments are no longer applicable. This talk is based on joint work with Guozhen Lu, Nguyen Lam, Nguyen Van Hoang, Debdip Ganguly, Joshua Flynn, and Lingxiao Zhang.","id":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4","capacityId":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4","name":"Sharp Quantitative Stability Estimates in Caffarelli-Kohn-Nirenberg Type Inequalities","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":706,"waitlistCapacityId":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-29T16:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-29T16:50:00.000Z","locationId":"53df18b7-15a9-4d2e-8f2c-9445f7309783","locationName":"115-B","locationCode":"115-B","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":7,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4","displayValue":"Anh Do","answers":["Anh Do"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4","displayValue":"10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"1ecb8ea2-8324-4728-99d8-3e4c7da59a30":{"speakerId":"1ecb8ea2-8324-4728-99d8-3e4c7da59a30","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"38a03e7c-8dfd-4b8e-8b5f-e957e45935b4","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df":{"code":"SCSibelDogruAkgolSESSION","description":"Oscillation theory for difference equations involving the full Hahn operator remains largely unaddressed in the literature, despite extensive work on the qualitative analysis of solutions. While significant progress has been made in spectral and asymptotic properties, dedicated oscillation criteria are notably absent. Historical research has focused on transform methods and solution techniques, such as the Fourier–Hahn transform for quantum models, rather than oscillatory behavior. Even recent advances—including the adaptation of the Asymptotic Iteration Method and applications of Nevanlinna theory—provide powerful tools yet do not directly confront oscillation phenomena. In this talk, we initiate the systematic study of oscillation for such equations by establishing foundational, classical oscillation theorems for the second-order case.","id":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df","capacityId":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df","name":"Emerging Works on Hahn Difference Equations and Oscillation","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":523,"waitlistCapacityId":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-28T14:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T14:50:00.000Z","locationId":"002ab162-a334-43ba-82fb-22118ca06cc8","locationName":"115-C","locationCode":"115-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":7,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df","displayValue":"Sibel Dogru Akgol","answers":["Sibel Dogru Akgol"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df","displayValue":"8 - Analysis","answers":["8 - Analysis"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"54183fd9-973a-4671-b145-4aefb8d1da02":{"speakerId":"54183fd9-973a-4671-b145-4aefb8d1da02","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"775e0bf7-8c1b-4029-b892-eb99de0436df","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886":{"code":"SCpreetipreetiSESSION","description":"Our work owes its origin to a recent note of Ram Murty in which he proved that all the zeros of the digamma function are irrational with at most one possible exception. We extend this investigation to higher-order polygamma functions.","id":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886","capacityId":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886","name":"Irrationality of Zeros of Polygamma Functions","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-29T14:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-29T15:10:00.000Z","locationId":"002ab162-a334-43ba-82fb-22118ca06cc8","locationName":"115-C","locationCode":"115-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":10,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886","displayValue":"preeti preeti","answers":["preeti preeti"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886","displayValue":"3 - Number Theory","answers":["3 - Number Theory"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"4fae16d2-8c67-4659-aa70-5bd2656404cd":{"speakerId":"4fae16d2-8c67-4659-aa70-5bd2656404cd","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"bed73a1a-d865-4e65-b0c5-bcc7fd079886","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"a8f839a5-eb8a-4a3d-9ad4-782c4314fa56":{"code":"SCTapateeSahooSESSION","description":"The notion of partial order plays a significant role in studying concepts such as positive elements, convex ideals, and ordered morphisms in algebraic systems. In this presentation, we introduce a partial order on modules over a nearring and explore its connections with matrix nearring. Due to the absence of one of the distributive properties in nearrings (compared to rings), the structural aspects of partial orders in nearrings and matrix nearrings become especially significant. We establish a characterization of the positive cone in a group nearring, analogous to the positive cone in a matrix nearring. Furthermore, we examine properties of convex ideals and the conditions associated with Archimedean orders in both matrix nearrings and group nearrings. These results give new developments into the structure of ordered nearrings.","id":"a8f839a5-eb8a-4a3d-9ad4-782c4314fa56","capacityId":"a8f839a5-eb8a-4a3d-9ad4-782c4314fa56","name":"Partial Order in Module Over a Matrix Nearring and a Group Nearring","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"a8f839a5-eb8a-4a3d-9ad4-782c4314fa56_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T14:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T14:50:00.000Z","locationId":"c7fdfa90-a4e