"The purpose of this paper is to propose an  algorithm to solve  $f$-fixed point problem ($f$FPP) for quasi-$\\\\phi_{E^*}^{*}$-$f$-nonexpansive mapping in the setting of two real Banach spaces, and prove that the sequence generated by the proposed iterative algorithm converges strongly to a solution of the $f$FPP. Finally, we give a numerical example to verify our result.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}","plainTextDescription":"The purpose of this paper is to propose an  algorithm to solve  $f$-fixed point problem ($f$FPP) for quasi-$\\phi_{E^*}^{*}$-$f$-nonexpansive mapping in the setting of two real Banach spaces, and prove that the sequence generated by the proposed iterative algorithm converges strongly to a solution of the $f$FPP. Finally, we give a numerical example to verify our result.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":false,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710":{"code":"FabioMartinelliSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Kinetically constrained models (KCMs) are interacting particle systems introduced in the '80s by condensed matter physicists. These models provide accessible stochastic systems with glassy-type dynamics. The key mechanism behind their complex evolution is dynamical facilitation, embedded via appropriate kinetic constraints. KCMs are tightly related to bootstrap percolation, a widely studied monotone cellular automaton. Recently, KCMs have inspired the construction of quantum spin chains to explore many-body localization in the absence of disorder. I will begin with a short review of KCMs' main features and the behavior of two popular models. Then, I will focus on the KCMs counterpart of the famous “universality problem” for two-dimensional bootstrap percolation. In particular, I will highlight the major new features that emerge for KCMs with respect to bootstrap percolation.</div>","id":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710","capacityId":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710","name":"Universality for Two Dimensional Kinetically Constrained Spin Models","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","capacity":523,"waitlistCapacityId":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T19:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T19:45:00.000Z","locationId":"bc81cad2-8b75-45cb-9355-a764c88805b2","locationName":"120-AB","locationCode":"120-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":14,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710","displayValue":"Fabio Martinelli","answers":["Fabio Martinelli"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710","displayValue":"11 - Mathematical Physics","answers":["11 - Mathematical Physics"]}},"richTextDescription":"Kinetically constrained models (KCMs) are interacting particle systems introduced in the '80s by condensed matter physicists. These models provide accessible stochastic systems with glassy-type dynamics. The key mechanism behind their complex evolution is dynamical facilitation, embedded via appropriate kinetic constraints. KCMs are tightly related to bootstrap percolation, a widely studied monotone cellular automaton. Recently, KCMs have inspired the construction of quantum spin chains to explore many-body localization in the absence of disorder. I will begin with a short review of KCMs' main features and the behavior of two popular models. Then, I will focus on the KCMs counterpart of the famous “universality problem” for two-dimensional bootstrap percolation. In particular, I will highlight the major new features that emerge for KCMs with respect to bootstrap percolation.","plainTextDescription":"Kinetically constrained models (KCMs) are interacting particle systems introduced in the '80s by condensed matter physicists. These models provide accessible stochastic systems with glassy-type dynamics. The key mechanism behind their complex evolution is dynamical facilitation, embedded via appropriate kinetic constraints. KCMs are tightly related to bootstrap percolation, a widely studied monotone cellular automaton. Recently, KCMs have inspired the construction of quantum spin chains to explore many-body localization in the absence of disorder. I will begin with a short review of KCMs' main features and the behavior of two popular models. Then, I will focus on the KCMs counterpart of the famous “universality problem” for two-dimensional bootstrap percolation. In particular, I will highlight the major new features that emerge for KCMs with respect to bootstrap percolation.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"63b9a648-ed50-4666-82a1-1c2b68eb28aa":{"speakerId":"63b9a648-ed50-4666-82a1-1c2b68eb28aa","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"7e926622-69ac-4dc2-8521-df17e2536710","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"d2a1e7f9-4219-48ae-9ae8-3b0c5321be84":{"code":"SCPankajSinghSESSION","description":"Using Mackey functors, we provide a general theory for classifyingtorsors of algebraic tori in terms of Brauer groups of finite fieldextensions of the base field.This generalizes Blunk's description of the tori 