uch as Catalan and Motzkin numbers, as well as new sequences arising from less symmetric classes.</div>","id":"5c962089-9ce5-4099-b9b1-b1e26a594ed8","capacityId":"5c962089-9ce5-4099-b9b1-b1e26a594ed8","name":"XXX CANCELLED Distinct Flattened Partitions Avoiding a Pattern of Length Four","status":7,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"5c962089-9ce5-4099-b9b1-b1e26a594ed8_waitlist","dataTagCode":"XXX CANCELLED Distinct Fl","startTime":"2026-07-25T15:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T15:50:00.000Z","locationId":"18f6e81f-a563-4e79-a907-872c0f1b59a7","locationName":"119-AB","locationCode":"119-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":0,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"5c962089-9ce5-4099-b9b1-b1e26a594ed8","displayValue":"13 - Combinatorics","answers":["13 - Combinatorics"]}},"richTextDescription":"In this talk, we explore pattern avoidance of length $4$ in distinct flattened partitions, extending previous work on length-3 patterns. We classify all $24$ patterns into Wilf-equivalence classes and determine the corresponding avoidance sequences. Using structural decompositions, generating function techniques, and the kernel method, we solve functional equations and derive explicit algebraic formulas. The resulting sequences include classical families such as Catalan and Motzkin numbers, as well as new sequences arising from less symmetric classes.","plainTextDescription":"In this talk, we explore pattern avoidance of length $4$ in distinct flattened partitions, extending previous work on length-3 patterns. We classify all $24$ patterns into Wilf-equivalence classes and determine the corresponding avoidance sequences. Using structural decompositions, generating function techniques, and the kernel method, we solve functional equations and derive explicit algebraic formulas. The resulting sequences include classical families such as Catalan and Motzkin numbers, as well as new sequences arising from less symmetric classes.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":false,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7":{"code":"SCAlexMassuccoSESSION","description":"In recent years, transformer architectures have revolutionized the field of language processing, opening the door to previously unforeseen possibilities. However, from a theoretical point of view, the mathematical models proposed in the literature often lack direct contact with the actual architectures and depend on strong simplifying assumptions. In this paper, we reduce this gap by modelling the data flow in multi-headed transformer architectures as time-dependent gradient flows for a suitable interaction energy capturing the design of the attention mechanism. The explicit dependence on time allows us to consider different weights for each head and for each layer, without imposing constraints on the initialization method. Moreover, we prove that the $\\omega$-limit of the gradient flows are stationary points of the interaction energy functional. Finally, we analyse the stability of the gradient flows considering perturbations of both the initial data and the weights. Specifically, on the one hand, we study the robustness of the proposed models with respect to noisy inputs, establishing a continuous dependence of the gradient flows on the initial data and uniqueness of the flows. On the other hand, we prove the $\\Gamma$-convergence of the perturbed interaction energy to the unperturbed one, leading to the convergence of the corresponding gradient flows.","id":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7","capacityId":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7","name":"Multi-Headed Transformer Architectures as Time-Dependent Wasserstein Gradient Flows","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":269,"waitlistCapacityId":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-24T14:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-24T15:10:00.000Z","locationId":"832094e7-0e57-4e55-8e2e-d69b459e2551","locationName":"116-A","locationCode":"116-A","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":30,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7","displayValue":"Alex Massucco","answers":["Alex Massucco"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7","displayValue":"14 - Mathematics of Computer Science","answers":["14 - Mathematics of Computer Science"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"2035f7ba-50e9-4856-afdc-da768d3f85c0":{"speakerId":"2035f7ba-50e9-4856-afdc-da768d3f85c0","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"be40438d-ce0a-406b-80d5-befdb8f109c7","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"29b6793b-a69b-4f9e-94dd-0221c4c7c700":{"code":"ICMFNCOFF15","description":"","id":"29b6793b-a69b-4f9e-94dd-0221c4c7c700","capacityId":"29b6793b-a69b-4f9e-94dd-