on Hunsicker, Senior Manager, Soccer analytics at Philadelphia Union soccer\r\nJames Gilman, Senior Director of Football Research and Strategy  at Philadelphia Eagles football\r\nModerator: Jordan Ellenberg\r\nLearn more by clicking here.","registrantInformation":"All Full Attendees","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"92b3b133-b28a-4586-ab02-075b6aeaeddf":{"speakerId":"92b3b133-b28a-4586-ab02-075b6aeaeddf","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":5},"165aeeb1-b5a0-4e89-b063-3ec4ef410504":{"speakerId":"165aeeb1-b5a0-4e89-b063-3ec4ef410504","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":6},"d60c6b99-a083-4cc9-8d90-f501da2a6035":{"speakerId":"d60c6b99-a083-4cc9-8d90-f501da2a6035","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":3},"d1dc23a2-57e2-4742-b9fa-deaf5c813dc9":{"speakerId":"d1dc23a2-57e2-4742-b9fa-deaf5c813dc9","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":2},"dc2b94c2-8197-4890-9d33-9cb77b7363ce":{"speakerId":"dc2b94c2-8197-4890-9d33-9cb77b7363ce","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":1},"9d845a07-6070-483c-8661-e6b65df081b3":{"speakerId":"9d845a07-6070-483c-8661-e6b65df081b3","speakerCategoryId":"aaac65e2-8724-4ec3-853a-7247189f454c","sessionId":"17a51b92-32c5-44d3-9859-9b7765bc4005","sessionSpeakerOrder":4}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-04-12T12:36:18.327Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411":{"code":"SCJędrzejGarnekSESSION","description":"The study of the cohomology of algebraic varieties equipped with an action of a finite group is a classical and well-developed area. In this talk, I present new results on the Hodge, de Rham, and crystalline cohomology of smooth projective curves endowed with an action of a finite $p$-group in characteristic $p$.I show that the Hodge and de Rham cohomologies of an arbitrary $p$-group cover admit a decomposition into a global part and local parts. The global part depends only on the 'topology' of the cover (i.e. the stabilizer groups and the quotient curve). The local parts arise from Harbater–Katz–Gabber covers that approximate the cover over a given point. This extends my earlier results for generic covers and generalizes a theorem of Nakajima in the étale case.I then address the analogous question for crystalline cohomology. Although such a decomposition is not known in general, I present a new result for $\\mathbb Z/p^n \\mathbb Z$-covers which provides evidence that a similar local–global phenomenon should hold in this setting as well.","id":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411","capacityId":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411","name":"Equivariant Structure of the Cohomologies of Curves","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T15:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T16:10:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":12,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411","displayValue":"Jędrzej Garnek","answers":["Jędrzej Garnek"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411","displayValue":"4 - Algebraic and Complex Geometry","answers":["4 - Algebraic and Complex Geometry"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"ec479321-9045-48d8-b5ad-318cc7cc7148":{"speakerId":"ec479321-9045-48d8-b5ad-318cc7cc7148","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"44e6a588-5128-455e-b915-f45668fd1411","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"ea88615a-33b0-49bf-8e6e-5642c6aef3e2":{"code":"SCKrishnaKumarGSESSION","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">According to the spectral theorem, the norm behavior of normal matrices can be completely determined from their spectrum; however, for non-normal matrices, the spectrum alone is not sufficient to describe their behavior. Determining the norm behavior of non-normal matrices from spectral-related sets is a fundamental problem in matrix theory. In this work, we prove that the pseudospectra and condition spectra completely characterize the norm behavior of Jordan matrices for any matrix $p$-norm. Furthermore, we provide sufficient conditions for determining the $1$-norm and $\\infty$-norm behavior of bidiagonal matrices based on their pseudospectra and condition spectra.</div>","id":"ea88615a-33b0-49bf-8e6e-5642c6aef3e2","capacityId":"ea88615a-33b0-49bf-8e6e-5642c6aef3e2","name":"Norm Behavior of Jordan and Bidiagonal Matrices","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"ea88615a-33b0-49bf-8e6e-5642c6aef3e2_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","start