functions on sigma-algebras, and to develop a limit theory for submodular setfunctions.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[{\"offset\":0,\"length\":42,\"style\":\"BOLD\"}],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}","plainTextDescription":"László Lovász is giving this Abel Lecture.\r\n\r\nIn the last 20 years, limit theories of sequences of large discrete structures (graphs, permutations, posets) have been developed. Finite graphs have associated matroids, and it is a natural task to describe what sort of convergence and limit objects for these matroids are implied by the convergence of a graph sequence. Matroids, introduced by Whitney in 1935, and more generally submodular setfunctions, provide common generalizations and transparent proofs of many combinatorial results in graph connectivity, flow theory, matching theory, the theory of rigidity of rameworks, and many other topics. To define appropriate limit objects, submodular setfunctions on infinite sets, in particular sigma-algebras, have to be considered. Such setfunctions are also important in analysis, going back to the seminal work of Choquet (1954) in the theory of electric capacity and the ``nonlinear integral''. These two research lines, however, have not had much interaction. The goal of the research surveyed in this paper is to describe meaningful analogies between important notions and results for finite matroids and submodular setfunctions on sigma-algebras, and to develop a limit theory for submodular setfunctions.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"1094afd1-2e96-451b-b5bd-496903ab3567":{"speakerId":"1094afd1-2e96-451b-b5bd-496903ab3567","speakerCategoryId":"2d7fd3da-0a3b-46f4-8afe-8773b72aa543","sessionId":"40d0b65c-81b4-445a-b8f2-9c51b1ac2344","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-10-04T09:07:49.363Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"741c9cbb-a111-441e-b721-c426d6e31566":{"code":"","description":"","id":"741c9cbb-a111-441e-b721-c426d6e31566","capacityId":"741c9cbb-a111-441e-b721-c426d6e31566","name":"Exhibition & Collaboration","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"71abacf4-0618-41d9-8582-c8312a3fc809","waitlistCapacityId":"741c9cbb-a111-441e-b721-c426d6e31566_waitlist","dataTagCode":"Exhibition & Collabo725","startTime":"2026-07-25T13:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T22:00:00.000Z","locationId":"ca98ab39-5039-43e6-934e-5e7aa254dd53","locationName":"Hall E - Expo","locationCode":"Hall E - Expo","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":83,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","b138550a-fdad-4b93-b3c1-08eca41a841f","5b4c0831-8b9d-484d-bd6c-214c48d235d1","eed7f846-6665-4f33-973a-69077e324c7c","460d73a8-a9a0-4af3-a523-6f426e8da92b","bff6831d-6419-4dbc-a0ed-839f5da7edfe","5cb4f53d-aabf-4bc3-b876-8db33cba9ba5","9e68476d-7aaa-434f-aa89-ad258c9770d3","75c39658-1230-4293-815d-ba4009d4c5fc","c3465557-42c5-4bd0-8672-db0f026639c8","5fc38934-8fb8-4dd0-9691-f0da68e36cd9"],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-04-12T10:57:23.667Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"db271251-fcc2-4f6f-a4c0-943e8978266f":{"code":"SCAparnaKovilakamSESSION","description":"The Gram-Schmidt process introduced by J.P. Gram and E. Schmidt enables one to orthonormalize a set of vectors in an inner product space. Using this, one can transform a square matrix to an orthogonal matrix by an upper triangular matrix. This motivated L.N. Vaserstein to introduce a similar method for symplectic matrices in 1976. For a given alternating matrix ϕ of size 2n, Vaserstein proved the existence of two elementary matrices of size 2n−1, which can be modified to get symplectic matrices with respect to ϕ. In our recent paper, we defined a set of matrices analogous to Vaserstein-type matrices, for Petrov’s odd unitary group, which is a generalization of all classical groups. We proved that these are elementary linear matrices, and these matrices belong to Petrov’s odd unitary group under some conditions. We also proved that these matrices generate Petrov’s odd elementary hyperbolic unitary group when the ring is commutative. Thus our construction gives a new set of generators for the odd elementary hyperbolic unitary group and thereby visualizing this group in a simpler form. We have also proved that the elementary matrices in the odd unitary analogue of the Vaserstein’s construction form a set of generators for the elementary linear group. I would like to discuss this result in detail.","id":"db271251-fcc2-4f6f-a4c0-943e8978266f","capacityId":"db271251-fcc2-4f6f-a4c0-943e8978266f","name":"On the Odd Unitary Analogue of Gram-Schmidt Process","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"db271251-fcc2-4f6f-a4c0-943e8978266f_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-28T15:30:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-28T15:50:00.000Z","locationId":"002ab162-a334-43ba-82fb-22118ca06cc8","locationName":"115-C","locationCode":"115-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":9,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"db271251-fcc2-4f6f-a4c0-943e8978266f","displayValue":"Aparna Pradeep Vadakke Kovilakam