al hyperbolic manifolds to consider thinking about discrete subgroups of higher rank Lie groups. To that end, we breezily discuss some applications of the ideas from the theory of Kleinian groups in the higher rank setting.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"7eeeb9f9-134c-4b54-87d5-b1f757b956fc":{"speakerId":"7eeeb9f9-134c-4b54-87d5-b1f757b956fc","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"8035a4f7-ca2b-4103-8c77-918befef6856","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"e33bbc16-4e8e-4528-a43d-432f136d8d44":{"code":"SCSarahNakatoSESSION","description":"An irreducible element of a commutative ring is called absolutely irreducible if none of its powers has more than one (essentially different) factorizations into irreducibles. Otherwise, the irreducible element is called non-absolutely irreducible.  While absolute irreducibility has been studied in various contexts, including the study of number fields and class groups, its investigation in the theory of non-unique factorization of domains is still limited.Not all absolutely irreducible elements are prime, and certain domains exhibit striking extremes: either all irreducibles are absolutely irreducible, or none are. In this talk, we discuss integral domains that realize each of the eight logically possible scenarios concerning the existence or non-existence of primes, absolutely irreducibles that are not prime, and irreducible elements that are not absolutely irreducible. These constructions highlight the diversity of factorization behavior in commutative algebra and give new insights into the structure of integral domains.","id":"e33bbc16-4e8e-4528-a43d-432f136d8d44","capacityId":"e33bbc16-4e8e-4528-a43d-432f136d8d44","name":"Primes and Absolutely or Non-Absolutely Irreducible Elements in Atomic Domains","status":2,"typ