6-2de3921636ad":{"code":"HongWangSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Projection theorems study how  Hausdorff dimension of fractal sets behaves under orthogonal projections from Euclidean space to its subspaces.  We will survey some of the recent results on orthogonal projections to restricted families of subspaces and their connections to restriction theory in harmonic analysis and incidence geometry in combinatorics.</div>","id":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad","capacityId":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad","name":"Restricted Orthogonal Projections","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","waitlistCapacityId":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T22:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T22:45:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":74,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad","displayValue":"Hong Wang","answers":["Hong Wang"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad","displayValue":"8 - Analysis,10 - Partial Differential Equations","answers":["8 - Analysis","10 - Partial Differential Equations"]}},"richTextDescription":"Projection theorems study how  Hausdorff dimension of fractal sets behaves under orthogonal projections from Euclidean space to its subspaces.  We will survey some of the recent results on orthogonal projections to restricted families of subspaces and their connections to restriction theory in harmonic analysis and incidence geometry in combinatorics.","plainTextDescription":"Projection theorems study how  Hausdorff dimension of fractal sets behaves under orthogonal projections from Euclidean space to its subspaces.  We will survey some of the recent results on orthogonal projections to restricted families of subspaces and their connections to restriction theory in harmonic analysis and incidence geometry in combinatorics.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"f3f0e790-5f4b-43b8-b9e4-1e10d30d5587":{"speakerId":"f3f0e790-5f4b-43b8-b9e4-1e10d30d5587","speakerCategoryId":"45ab9016-e39c-4327-8bdb-50c0e5f04916","sessionId":"a82ce84d-a9c9-447f-aa16-2de3921636ad","sessionSpeakerOrder":1}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361":{"code":"SCMohamedMnifSESSION","description":"Developing algorithms for solving Hamilton Jacobi Bellman Equations (HJB in short) in high dimension has been a difficult task for a long time, due to the difficulty known as the “curse of dimensionality”. A classical and efficient algorithm used to solve HJB equations is the Howard algorithm. It consists in computing two sequences: the optimal strategy and the value function which is the solution of a linear system. The computational cost for solving discrete system  based on the whole grid goes up exponentially with the dimensionality. This presentation introduces a deep learning-based approach that can handle a high-dimensional HJB related to optimal portfolio choice. The problem consists on an investor who begins with an initial endowment and wants to maximize the expected utility of his terminal wealth when he trades between a large number of risky assets. For solving the associated Hamilton Jacobi Bellman Equation, we employed a power transformation which leads to a semi linear parabolic equation. Under some conditions the existence of a smooth solution was proved and then the optimal portfolio was characterized. The optimal investment strategy is obtained in a feedback form by solving an optimization problem under constraints whose criterion is dependent on the derivative of the value function. The advantage of our approach is to make a connection between the semi linear PDE a BSDE with Lipschitz generator w.r.t $(Y,Z)$ which is solved by  two algorithms based on  deep neural networks.  We use two neural networks. The first one approximates the optimal strategy. The second one is used for the value function and its gradient. On each time step, we propose to use some machine learning techniques to estimate the optimal portfolio and separately the solution of the PDE and its gradient for the first numerical scheme or simultaneously the solution of the PDE and its gradient for the second numerical scheme. We minimize a loss function defined recursively by backward induction, and we solve the local problems by a stochastic gradient algorithm. We provide error estimates in terms of the universal approximation of neural networks. We compare the results of the two algorithms with a third one called direct algorithm learning control which consists in computing the control still with a neural network maximizing directly the expectation of the utility of the terminal wealth. Numerical experiments illustrate the efficiency of these algorithms.","id":"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361","capacityId":"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361","name":"XXX CANCELLED Deep Backward Schemes for High-Dimensional Portfolio Optimization Problem","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":629,"waitlistCapacityId":"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-26T17:50:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-26T18:10:00.000Z","locationId":"01c33352-9c21-48c8-9c87-4a12ea950167","locationName":"118-AB","locationCode":"118-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":0,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361","displayValue":"Mohamed Mnif","answers":["Mohamed Mnif"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"ff5e50dd-c153-462e-8128-f59471bad361","displayValue":"16 - Control Theory and Optimization","answers":["16 - Control Theory and Optimization"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":false,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"00f923e8-8d51-4cd8-8635-67bacc4a79c6":{"code":"WojciechSamotijSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Even though entropy methods have been used in combinatorics for at least five decades, only in recent years has their use really proliferated. There are now tens, if not hundreds, of combinatorial papers that crucially rely on the notion of entropy and exploit the various powerful identities and inequalities relating entropies. We attempt to give a broad overview of these works, outlining some of the key ideas.</div>","id":"00f923e8-8d51-4cd8-8635-67bacc4a79c6","capacityId":"00f923e8-8d51-4cd8-8635-67bacc4a79c6","name":"Entropy Methods in Combinatorics","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"7ac6a87e-8daa-4742-9215-4437219ac29a","capacity":706,"waitlistCapa