dictive Models for Breast Cancer Brain Metastasis: An Integration of Neuroimaging and Genomics Data for Better Clinical Practice\" by Chipo Zidana (17 - Statistics, Machine Learning, Image and Signal Processing)","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-06-29T17:53:32.093Z","restrictSessionVisibility":false},"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b":{"code":"SCXinruiYouSESSION","description":"Bernstein and Gelfand classified the projective functors (i.e. direct summands of the tensor by a finite dimensional representation) on the category of Z-finite modules on 1980. We will discuss the restriction of the projective functors on the category O, and the connection to Soergel bimodules.","id":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b","capacityId":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b","name":"The Projective Functors on the Category O","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-24T17:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-24T17:30:00.000Z","locationId":"bc81cad2-8b75-45cb-9355-a764c88805b2","locationName":"120-AB","locationCode":"120-AB","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":16,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b","displayValue":"Xinrui You","answers":["Xinrui You"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b","displayValue":"7 - Lie Theory and Generalizations","answers":["7 - Lie Theory and Generalizations"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"ac575e75-41d6-447b-94bc-49b38b391094":{"speakerId":"ac575e75-41d6-447b-94bc-49b38b391094","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"20e7eddb-175c-4f37-94c0-0612323c431b","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:34:17.600Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000":{"code":"SCEmilyMcMillonSESSION","description":"Given a graph $G$ and a target graph $H$, an $H$-coloring of $G$ is an adjacency-preserving vertex map from $G$ to $H$. By appropriate choice of $H$, these colorings can express, for instance, the independent sets or proper vertex colorings of $G$. Sidorenko proved that for any $H$, the $n$-vertex star admits at least as many $H$-colorings as any other $n$-vertex tree, but the minimization question remains open in general. For many graphs $H$, path graphs are among the trees with the fewest $H$-colorings, but work of Leontovich and subsequently Csikvari and Lin shows that there is a graph $E_7$ on seven vertices and a target graph $H$ for which there are strictly fewer $H$-colorings of $E_7$ than of the path on seven vertices.We introduce a new strategy for enumerating homomorphisms from path-like trees to highly symmetric target graphs that allows us to make the previous observations completely explicit and extend them to infinitely many $n$ beyond $n=7$. In particular, we exhibit a target graph $H$ with the property that for each sufficiently large $n$, there is a tree $E_n$ on $n$ vertices that admits strictly fewer $H$-colorings than the path on $n$ vertices.","id":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000","capacityId":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000","name":"Long Paths Need Not Minimize $H$-Colorings Among Trees","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","waitlistCapacityId":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-25T16:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-25T16:30:00.000Z","locationId":"6e51c4bd-da66-4c88-ad85-d7820995d66d","locationName":"118-C","locationCode":"118-C","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":13,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":[],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b":{"id":"1ff8930b-09e9-45ce-993d-1226c743588b","sessionId":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000","displayValue":"Emily McMillon","answers":["Emily McMillon"]},"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000","displayValue":"13 - Combinatorics","answers":["13 - Combinatorics"]}},"registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":true,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":true,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{"f198d390-2ec0-4ad0-bf85-28fc02cfea0b":{"speakerId":"f198d390-2ec0-4ad0-bf85-28fc02cfea0b","speakerCategoryId":"ab3cd2dc-d1f9-4200-a5a7-26b170cec2fb","sessionId":"df897d90-b174-4735-973b-254720874000","sessionSpeakerOrder":0}},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2026-05-16T11:49:56.663Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"a16b897a-aab4-44f9-9ccd-2ef0bc8af64c":{"code":"CarlosLópez LeivaSession","description":"<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">My goal in this session is to introduce the work done with bilingual (Spanish and English) students exploring, animating, and expressing mathematical concepts. The expressions and concepts that we have experienced in the bilingual classroom have helped us note that wonder as much as languaging are essential in the process of embodying mathematical concepts and feeling our connections to them and ourselves. Furthermore, in this process, I will conceptualize how the assemblages formed among students, objects, adults, and expressions—embedded in the physical and discursive animation of mathematical concepts—move through a translanguaging corriente or stream, which is nurtured through a mutual noticing and being with each other in the here and now. The sharing of our experiences facilitating and doing this work will be used as a platform to engage the participants in sharing their expressions on how this approach resonates with them and their contexts.</div>","id":"a16b897a-aab4-44f9-9ccd-2ef0bc8af64c","capacityId":"a16b897a-aab4-44f9-9ccd-2ef0bc8af64c","name":"XX Promoting Wonderment and Translanguaging in Mathematics: Conceptual Explorations","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"5a463645-2911-414f-bd81-e60ed23dd86b","waitlistCapacityId":"a16b897a-aab4-44f9-9ccd-2ef0bc8af64c_waitlist","dataTagCode":"XX Promoting Wonderment a","startTime":"2026-07-23T12:00:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-30T13:00:00.000Z","isIncludedSession":false,"isWaitlistEnabled":false,"registeredCount":0,"associatedWithAdmissionItems":[],"availableToAdmissionItems":[],"associatedRegistrationTypes":["00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","b138550a-fdad-4b93-b3c1-08eca41a841f","5b4c0831-8b9d-484d-bd6c-214c48d235d1","460d73a8-a9a0-4af3-a523-6f426e8da92b","bff6831d-6419-4dbc-a0ed-839f5da7edfe","9e68476d-7aaa-434f-aa89-ad258c9770d3","c3465557-42c5-4bd0-8672-db0f026639c8","5fc38934-8fb8-4dd0-9691-f0da68e36cd9","34cd407d-1544-459e-800a-f806dbbc59e1"],"sessionCustomFieldValues":{"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203":{"id":"ed161947-576f-4a6a-a4e1-5d22ce5a1203","sessionId":"a16b897a-aab4-44f9-9ccd-2ef0bc8af64c","displayValue":"19 - Mathematical Education and Popularization of Mathematics","answers":["19 - Mathematical Education and Popularization of Mathematics"]}},"richTextDescription":"My goal in this session is to introduce the work done with bilingual (Spanish and English) students exploring, animating, and expressing mathematical concepts. The expressions and concepts that we have experienced in the bilingual classroom have helped us note that wonder as much as languaging are essential in the process of embodying mathematical concepts and feeling our connections to them and ourselves. Furthermore, in this process, I will conceptualize how the assemblages formed among students, objects, adults, and expressions—embedded in the physical and discursive animation of mathematical concepts—move through a translanguaging corriente or stream, which is nurtured through a mutual noticing and being with each other in the here and now. The sharing of our experiences facilitating and doing this work will be used as a platform to engage the participants in sharing their expressions on how this approach resonates with them and their contexts.","plainTextDescription":"My goal in this session is to introduce the work done with bilingual (Spanish and English) students exploring, animating, and expressing mathematical concepts. The expressions and concepts that we have experienced in the bilingual classroom have helped us note that wonder as much as languaging are essential in the process of embodying mathematical concepts and feeling our connections to them and ourselves. Furthermore, in this process, I will conceptualize how the assemblages formed among students, objects, adults, and expressions—embedded in the physical and discursive animation of mathematical concepts—move through a translanguaging corriente or stream, which is nurtured through a mutual noticing and being with each other in the here and now. The sharing of our experiences facilitating and doing this work will be used as a platform to engage the participants in sharing their expressions on how this approach resonates with them and their contexts.","registrantInformation":"","displayPriority":0,"showOnAgenda":false,"isOpenForAttendeeHub":false,"featuredSession":false,"speakerIds":{},"documents":{},"createdDate":"2025-04-11T14:49:56.810Z","restrictSessionVisibility":true},"c0b646fc-e6f9-4f26-88c8-8582efd473a2":{"code":"SCDemianNahuelGoosSESSION","description":"This talk presents a detailed analysis of the circumstances that lead to Georg Cantor’s first paper on set theory in 1874. Particular attention is paid to Cantor’s correspondence with Richard Dedekind and the exchange of ideas between the two scholars. The undisclosed use of remarks of Dedekind’s in Cantor’s 1874 paper led to tensions and a tacit dispute over the authorship of the mathematical results contained therein. In this context, this talk announces the discovery of the Dedekind letters of Cantor’s Nachlass, which were believed to be lost. Two of those unedited letters from 1873 were used to confirm that some of the mathematical results in Cantor's 1874 paper were due to Dedekind. This matter, although very probable, had not yet been definitively confirmed and has led to greater tensions among historians who have dealt with Cantor and Dedekind.Further analyses of the relationship between the two mathematicians, the development of the mathematical ideas, and the authorship of the 1874 article are presented.","id":"c0b646fc-e6f9-4f26-88c8-8582efd473a2","capacityId":"c0b646fc-e6f9-4f26-88c8-8582efd473a2","name":"The Grandeur and the Sorrow Behind the First Set-Theoretical Paper and the Emergence of the Dedekind Letters","status":2,"type":"Session","defaultFeeId":"00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000","fees":{},"closedReasonType":"NotClosed","isOpenForRegistration":false,"presentationType":"","categoryId":"dd348cad-9fa9-4bfb-8f6b-0d76eda0f5fe","capacity":346,"waitlistCapacityId":"c0b646fc-e6f9-4f26-88c8-8582efd473a2_waitlist","dataTagCode":"","startTime":"2026-07-24T16:10:00.000Z","endTime":"2026-07-24T16:30:00.000Z","locatio