                "fcece601-0b9d-4ef9-847f-b07b0be3d067": {
                    "code": "SCAjayKumarSESSION",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">In this talk, we introduce a novel discrete neuron map by coupling a cosine-based memristor into a reduced Chialvo neuron map to investigate the dynamical effects of electromagnetic radiation on neuron dynamics. The nonlinear current-voltage characteristics of the memristor enrich the map's behavior, leading to diverse firing regimes, stability behavior, and chaotic attractors with fractal dimensions. This study starts with examining the equilibrium points using theoretical and numerical methods. Also, we determine the conditions on parameters under which the proposed map undergoes a Neimark-Sacker (NS) bifurcation, and further numerical investigation reveals the antimonotonicity structure through both forward and backward bifurcation diagrams. This map exhibits a range of codimension-one and -two bifurcation patterns, including Neimark-Sacker, period-doubling, saddle-node, generalized period-doubling, cusp-point, fold-flip, and various resonance structures (1:1, 1:2, 1:3, and 1:4). We observe that the coexistence of a stable limit cycle, a period-five attractor, and a chaotic attractor, all along with their respective basin of attraction regions. Furthermore, we extend this analysis to the network of neurons under the ring-star configuration and discuss several spatiotemporal patterns. This network investigation shows complex collective behaviors, including imperfect synchronization, clustered patterns, and multi-chimera state phenomena not previously observed in existing Chialvo-based studies. These results highlight the potential of the discrete memristor-based neuron map for advancing theoretical neurodynamics and offer a robust framework for investigating low-dimensional yet dynamically rich neuron systems.</div>",
