                    "richTextDescription": "{\"format\":\"draftjs-nucleus\",\"version\":1,\"content\":{\"blocks\":[{\"key\":\"9tu6l\",\"text\":\"From a practical point of view, continuous first-order logic provides us with a framework for the model-theoretic study of metric structures.  From a more abstract point of view, it also raises quite naturally the question of what should non-commutative continuous logic be?\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[{\"offset\":238,\"length\":15,\"style\":\"ITALIC\"}],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}},{\"key\":\"4hr1h\",\"text\":\"This is an intentionally vague question that lends itself to more than one interpretation.  One way to understand it is to observe that the algebra of continuous formulas forms (the self-adjoint part of) a commutative, unital C*-algebra.  Can we drop « commutative » here?  What should that even mean?\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[{\"offset\":227,\"length\":1,\"style\":\"SUPERSCRIPT\"}],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}},{\"key\":\"755fd\",\"text\":\"Ideas coming from recent developments in affine logic finally allow us to propose an answer to this question, giving rise to a logic that is strongly reminiscent of the Dirac--von Neumann formalism for observables in quantum mechanics.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}},{\"key\":\"a22vq\",\"text\":\"\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{}}}",
                    "plainTextDescription": "From a practical point of view, continuous first-order logic provides us with a framework for the model-theoretic study of metric structures.  From a more abstract point of view, it also raises quite naturally the question of what should non-commutative continuous logic be?\r\nThis is an intentionally vague question that lends itself to more than one interpretation.  One way to understand it is to observe that the algebra of continuous formulas forms (the self-adjoint part of) a commutative, unital C*-algebra.  Can we drop « commutative » here?  What should that even mean?\r\nIdeas coming from recent developments in affine logic finally allow us to propose an answer to this question, giving rise to a logic that is strongly reminiscent of the Dirac--von Neumann formalism for observables in quantum mechanics.",
