                    "richTextDescription": "{\"format\":\"draftjs-nucleus\",\"version\":1,\"content\":{\"blocks\":[{\"key\":\"6v9jc\",\"text\":\"Step into history with a special visit to Bryn Mawr College, where Karen Parshall will give a talk about groundbreaking mathematician, Emmy Noether and how she spent the final chapter of her remarkable life.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}},{\"key\":\"96vaq\",\"text\":\"In 1933, the German algebraist Emmy Noether (1882-1935) joined the Department of Mathematics at Bryn Mawr College. Renowned as one of the guiding lights behind what became known as modern algebra, Noether had overcome many obstacles. As a woman, she was able to earn a doctoral degree in Germany only after a law was passed 1908.Then, as a woman, she was denied the right to apply for the credential that would have allowed her to teach in 1915.Only thanks to the intervention of two heavyhitters--- Felix Klein and David Hilbert-did she finally succeed in getting a paid teaching job in 1923. Ten years later, however, the Nazis revoked the right to teach for all Jews. Friends in the United States secured her the position at Bryn Mawr that she held for less than two years, owing to her untimely death.\",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[],\"data\":{}},{\"key\":\"5onk7\",\"text\":\"Learn more click here. \",\"type\":\"unstyled\",\"depth\":0,\"inlineStyleRanges\":[],\"entityRanges\":[{\"offset\":11,\"length\":10,\"key\":0}],\"data\":{}}],\"entityMap\":{\"0\":{\"type\":\"LINK\",\"mutability\":\"MUTABLE\",\"data\":{\"type\":\"WEB_ADDRESS\",\"url\":\"https://www.icm2026.org/4oEWoO\",\"subjectLine\":\"\",\"target\":\"_blank\",\"errorContent\":[],\"errorInLinkText\":false,\"errorInLinkUrl\":false}}}}}",
                    "plainTextDescription": "Step into history with a special visit to Bryn Mawr College, where Karen Parshall will give a talk about groundbreaking mathematician, Emmy Noether and how she spent the final chapter of her remarkable life.\r\nIn 1933, the German algebraist Emmy Noether (1882-1935) joined the Department of Mathematics at Bryn Mawr College. Renowned as one of the guiding lights behind what became known as modern algebra, Noether had overcome many obstacles. As a woman, she was able to earn a doctoral degree in Germany only after a law was passed 1908.Then, as a woman, she was denied the right to apply for the credential that would have allowed her to teach in 1915.Only thanks to the intervention of two heavyhitters--- Felix Klein and David Hilbert-did she finally succeed in getting a paid teaching job in 1923. Ten years later, however, the Nazis revoked the right to teach for all Jews. Friends in the United States secured her the position at Bryn Mawr that she held for less than two years, owing to her untimely death.\r\nLearn more click here.",
