                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Let \\( C(X) \\) denote the lattice-ordered algebra of real-valued continuous functions on a realcompact space \\( X \\). Given a function \\( \\psi \\in C(\\mathbb{R}) \\), we say that a bounded, identity-preserving linear functional \\( h \\) on \\( C(X) \\) is a \\(\\psi\\)-homomorphism if it satisfies\\[h \\circ \\psi = \\psi \\circ h.\\]A function \\( \\psi \\) is said to be realcompact if every \\(\\psi\\)-homomorphism from \\( C(X) \\) to \\( \\mathbb{R} \\) is simply an evaluation at a point of \\( X \\).Classical results by Hewitt and Shirota established that the square and the absolute value functions are realcompact. In this talk, we extend these results by offering a broader characterization of such functions. Quite surprisingly, it turns out that a function \\( \\psi \\in C(\\mathbb{R}) \\) is realcompact if and only if it is non-affine.This characterization also leads to a Banach--Stone type result linking the structure of function algebras to the topology of the underlying realcompact spaces.</div>",
