                "53b5c4ca-bf2e-4c6a-8232-751f02e21329": {
                    "code": "SCAnalenMalnegroSESSION",
                    "description": "An edge-coloring of a graph $G$ is an assignment of colors to the edges of $G$, with one color assigned to each edge. If adjacent edges are assigned distinct colors, then the edge-coloring is said to be a proper edge-coloring. A strong edge-coloring of a graph $G$ is a proper edge-coloring in which edges at distance at most $2$ receive distinct colors. The minimum number of colors required for a strong edge-coloring of a graph $G$ is called the strong chromatic index, denoted by $\\chi_s'(G)$. In this paper, the authors investigate the strong chromatic index of certain special graphs and of the join of two graphs.",
