                "4a5f5fdf-6d64-4a9a-b360-6bb95c99a4db": {
                    "code": "SCDipankarGhoshSESSION",
                    "description": "In this article, we analyze how (projective and injective) complexity, curvature, and complete intersection dimension behave under linkage of modules and ideals. Let $R$ be a Gorenstein local ring. Consider a Gorenstein perfect ideal $\\mathfrak{a}$ (e.g., $\\mathfrak{a}$ is generated by an $R$-regular sequence). Let $M$ and $N$ be two Cohen-Macaulay $R$-modules linked by $\\mathfrak{a}$. We prove that ${\\rm cx}_R(M) = {\\rm injcx}_R(N)$ and ${\\rm curv}_R(M) = {\\rm injcurv}_R(N)$. In particular, when $R$ is complete intersection, ${\\rm cx}_R(M) = {\\rm cx}_R(N)$ and ${\\rm curv}_R(M) = {\\rm curv}_R(N)$. Furthermore, we show that projective dimension ${\\rm pd}_R(M)={\\rm pd}_R(N)$, and complete intersection dimension ${\\rm cid}_R(M)={\\rm cid}_R(N)$. If any of these dimensions is finite, it is equal to height of $\\mathfrak{a}$. Similar results are obtained for linkage of ideals. All these results highly extend a classical result of Peskine and Szpiro in many directions. We construct several examples that complement our results. These also show how properties like `integrally closed', `$\\mathfrak{m}$-full' and `Burch' behave under linkage of ideals.    This is a joint work with Subhadip Bhowmick.",
                    "id": "4a5f5fdf-6d64-4a9a-b360-6bb95c99a4db",
