                "f4e92d54-b234-4798-b972-045a0fd87409": {
                    "code": "SCMohdAlmieBinAliasSESSION",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">In this study, we consider a competition system describing two populations. Starting from given initial values of the total tumor cell population, we aim to delay the onset of treatment failure, defined as the time at which one population dominates the tumor while the other is driven to extinction. We formulate optimal control problem to determine this critical time. The proposed formulations seek a balance between reducing treatment intensity and minimizing the overall tumor size, while simultaneously maximizing the competition between populations, thereby delaying competition exclusion of one of the populations. We compare the results between the model with the state-of-the-art continuous therapy and the recently proposed adaptive therapy.</div>",
