                "043c7384-d027-4494-8927-553467cc7387": {
                    "code": "SCElifSegahOztasSESSION",
                    "description": "DNA sequences consist of gene regions known as exons and introns. Exon regions are spliced and directly participate in protein synthesis, whereas intron regions are non-coding segments that are excluded from this process. In our previous work, we introduced a novel coding-theoretic model for the mathematical representation of genomic regions and validated its effectiveness on a limited dataset. In the present study, we extend this model by proposing an improved version of the algorithm and by conducting a comprehensive evaluation on a significantly larger dataset. The proposed algorithm models DNA segments as algebraic codes and examines their structural parameters, with particular emphasis on error-correction properties.Our results reveal that genomic regions corresponding to introns are consistently mapped to algebraic codes with zero error-correction capability. This outcome provides a mathematically precise criterion for distinguishing non-coding regions and demonstrates that algebraic invariants from coding theory offer a robust analytical tool for genomic classification.***Acknowledgements:The author Elif Segah OZTAS would like to acknowledge the contribution of the COST Action CA21169, supported by COST (European Cooperation in Science and Technology).",
