                "ffcbbaaf-f756-407d-b4e8-10f210df6d0c": {
                    "code": "SCKirillKapitonetsSESSION",
                    "description": "The Hardy function $Z(t)=\\zeta(1/2+it)e^{i\\theta(t)}$ takes real values for real $t$ and its real zeros are zeros $\\zeta(s)$ on the critical line $1/2+it$.<br />After discovering the critical value of the local maximum in 1956, Lehmer formulated the assumption that the Hardy function could have a negative local maximum or a positive local minimum.<br /><br />The paper proves the statement about the transformation of the zeros of the Hardy function, which doubts the Lehmer phenomenon:<br />I) the zeros of the Hardy function can be obtained by transforming the zeros of the function $\\cos\\theta(t)$;<br />II) all zeros of the Hardy function obtained by transformation are real;<br />III) the Hardy function has no other zeros except those obtained by transformation.<br /><br />In the course of the proof, several expressions were obtained that had not previously been considered by other researchers of the Zeta function:<br />1) Summation of the Zeta function based on the properties of partial sums of the Dirichlet series to form an almost regular polygon on the complex plane;<br />2) Definition and summation of the generalized Hardy function as the real part of the Hardy function on any line parallel critical line;<br />3) A property of the generalized Hardy function is established, which makes it possible to associate its zeros with the zeros of the function $\\cos\\theta(t)$;<br />4) Based on the binomial series, an expression is obtained for the transformation of a generalized Hardy function between any two lines parallel to a critical line.It is shown that the real zeros of the functions obtained by the transformation of the generalized Hardy function form either a strictly decreasing or strictly increasing sequence.<br />The interval between the values $\\sigma=1/2$ and $\\sigma = \\hat\\alpha_\\lambda$ is divided into $\\mu_\\lambda$ intervals and it is shown by induction that the zeros of the generalized Hardy function on any line parallel critical line, including the most critical line, are real.<br /><br />It is shown that the number of zeros of the Hardy function obtained by transformation from the function $\\cos\\theta(t)$ matches with the number of zeros $\\zeta(s)$ in the region of the critical strip with a height equal to the interval on which we calculated the number of zeros of the function $\\cos\\theta(t)$, therefore, the Hardy function has no other zeros in this interval.",
                    "id": "ffcbbaaf-f756-407d-b4e8-10f210df6d0c",
                    "capacityId": "ffcbbaaf-f756-407d-b4e8-10f210df6d0c",
