                "0b9cacf1-7709-44f8-b959-8a668bbd27e6": {
                    "code": "SCAnamikaPandeySESSION",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Transonic flows play a fundamental role in the theory of compressible Euler equations, as they involve the emergence of sonic curves along which the governing system loses strict hyperbolicity and undergoes a change in type. This degeneracy near sonic boundaries makes the analysis of such flows particularly subtle and mathematically challenging, while also being of practical relevance in high-speed aerodynamics and related applications where smooth transonic transitions are essential.  In this work, we investigate supersonic transonic phenomena for the two-dimensional compressible Euler equations governed by a polytropic van der Waals equation of state. In contrast to the ideal gas case, the non-ideal pressure law strengthens nonlinear effects and alters the degeneracy structure near sonic states, significantly complicating the analytical treatment. Working within a self-similar framework associated with a four-state Riemann problem, we construct a supersonic sonic patch solution that connects a strictly supersonic flow region to a sonic boundary along a pseudo streamline. The analysis is based on a characteristic decomposition combined with a partial hodograph transformation, through which the problem is reformulated as a degenerate hyperbolic system.  We establish the global existence of a supersonic solution in the self-similar plane and prove its uniform regularity up to the sonic curve. In addition, we investigate the regularity properties of the resulting sonic boundary. Our results extend the theory of supersonic sonic patches from polytropic ideal gases to a physically more realistic non-ideal gas model.</div>",
                    "id": "0b9cacf1-7709-44f8-b959-8a668bbd27e6",
                    "capacityId": "0b9cacf1-7709-44f8-b959-8a668bbd27e6",
