                "fa2a5f55-3823-4a96-8a80-0f7978b5a657": {
                    "code": "RichardSamworthSession",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">Traditionally, we think of statistical methods as being divided into parametric approaches, which can be restrictive, but where estimation is typically straightforward (e.g.~using maximum likelihood), and nonparametric methods, which are more flexible but often require careful choices of tuning parameters.  Nonparametric inference under shape constraints sits somewhere in the middle, seeking in some ways the best of both worlds.  I will provide a historical overview of the field, as well as a perspective on its current state, concluding with some thoughts on future directions and open problems.</div>",
                    "id": "fa2a5f55-3823-4a96-8a80-0f7978b5a657",
