                "412a1f69-5637-4752-9f4e-a8ca7e9c7b9d": {
                    "code": "DavidBen-ZviSessionJoint",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\"><div class=css-vsf5of><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">Classical harmonic analysis describes the decomposition of vector spaces of functions under the action of symmetries. In recent decades, a rich categorical harmonic analysis has emerged in which vector spaces of functions are replaced by categories of sheaves. We will explore some recent developments in this theory, centered around the search for a Fourier self-dual or symplectic form of categorical representation theory. This is<span style=\"color: rgb(0,0,0);\"> inspired by the physics of 3d mirror symmetry, the representation theory of double affine Hecke algebras and genuine equivariance in algebraic topology.&nbsp; The lecture is b</span>ased on ongoing joint work with Germán Stefanich.</p><p class=\"carina-rte-public-DraftStyleDefault-block\">&nbsp;</p></div></div>",
