                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">When I started my Ph.D., I had never even heard of combinatorics.  Adriano Garsia and Jeff Remmel eagerly stepped in and taught me that it is the study of a remarkable family of symmetric functions called Macdonald polynomials. By the time I discovered that their definition was not universally accepted, I had already been thoroughly indoctrinated. In this talk, I will share the Garsia–Remmel lessons, along with the story of how decades of playing with Young tableaux led to the development of a combinatorial architecture that underlies both these Macdonald polynomials and a branch of algebraic geometry known as Schubert calculus.</div>",
