                "1e4e121c-0e82-49d8-956d-5a6174f1aecb": {
                    "code": "SCMamataNandkeolyarSESSION",
                    "description": "<div class=\"ag87-crtemvc-hsbk\">This study presents a numerical analysis of the unsteady three-dimensional flow of a Casson fluid over a bi-directional stretching surface, considering the effects of Hall current, thermal radiation, homogeneous-heterogeneous reactions, viscous dissipation, and an external magnetic field. The flow is induced by the bi-directional stretching of the sheet in its plane. The presence of two different species in the flow region, participating in homogeneous and heterogeneous reactions, adds complexity to the problem. The fluid velocity, thermal, and mass transport are modelled using a set of nonlinear partial differential equations (PDEs) with appropriate boundary conditions. These PDEs are transformed into a set of nonlinear ordinary differential equations (ODEs) using similarity transformations. The transformed ODEs are then solved numerically using the bvp4c solver. The study presents a detailed analysis of the impact of various flow parameters on velocities, temperature, and concentration profiles through graphical representations. The results indicate that the thermal boundary layer thickness increases with higher Hall current, magnetic field strength, and thermal radiation. Conversely, mass transport decreases with higher homogeneous-heterogeneous reaction parameters. The study also calculates the physical quantities such as the coefficients of skin friction and heat and mass transfer.</div>",
